#lang: en

#comment: PicoDoc Tutorial — Getting Started
#comment: A progressive guide from minimal document to productive use.

#title: Getting Started with PicoDoc

#h2: What is PicoDoc?

PicoDoc is a macro-based markup language that compiles to HTML. Everything in
PicoDoc (headings, bold text, links, user-defined templates) is a macro
call. This single abstraction keeps the language regular and predictable\: once
you learn the macro syntax, you know the entire language.

#h2: Installation

PicoDoc requires [#b : Python 3.14+] and [#url link="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/" text="uv"].

#code: """
    git clone <repo-url> picodoc
    cd picodoc
    uv sync
    """

Verify the installation\:

#code: """
    uv run picodoc --help
    """

#h2: Your First Document

Create a file called [#code"hello.pdoc"]\:

#code: """
    #title: Hello, PicoDoc

    This is my first document.
    """

Compile it to HTML\:

#code: """
    uv run picodoc hello.pdoc -o hello.html
    """

Open [#code"hello.html"] in your browser. You should see a heading and a
paragraph. The bare text was automatically wrapped in a [#code"<p>"] tag
because PicoDoc treats any text not inside a macro call as an implicit
paragraph.

#h2: Headings and Structure

PicoDoc supports six levels of headings\:

#code: """
    #title: Level 1 (Document Title)
    #h2: Level 2 (Section)
    #h3: Level 3 (Subsection)
    #h4: Level 4
    #h5: Level 5
    #h6: Level 6
    """

All six levels have dash aliases that are shorter to type\:

#code: """
    #-: Level 1 (same as #title)
    #--: Level 2 (same as #h2)
    #---: Level 3 (same as #h3)
    #----: Level 4 (same as #h4)
    #-----: Level 5 (same as #h5)
    #------: Level 6 (same as #h6)
    """

Use [#code"#hr"] for a horizontal rule and [#code"#comment"] for text that
should not appear in the output\:

#code: """
    #hr

    #comment: This is a note to myself — not rendered.
    """

#h2: Paragraphs

Bare text is automatically wrapped in [#code"<p>"] tags. A blank line
separates paragraphs\:

#code: """
    This is the first paragraph. It can span
    multiple lines.

    This is the second paragraph.
    """

You can also use [#code"#p"] explicitly. The paragraph body form lets you
write the body on the following lines\:

#code: """
    #p: An explicit single-line paragraph.

    #p:
    A multi-line paragraph using
    the paragraph body form.
    """

#h2: Inline Formatting

#h3: Bold and Italic

Use \#b (or its alias \#**) for bold and \#i (or \#__) for italic\:

#code: """
    This is #**"bold" text.
    This is #__"italic" text.
    """

For longer spans, use the bracketed form\:

#code: """
    This is [#b : bold text that spans several words].
    This is [#i : italic text in brackets].
    """

Bold and italic can be nested\:

#code: """
    This is [#b : bold with [#i : italic inside]].
    """

#h3: Links

Use [#code"#url"] with [#code"link"] and [#code"text"] arguments\:

#code: """
    Visit [#url link="https://example.com" text="Example Site"] today.
    """

You can also use the body form for the link text\:

#code: """
    Visit [#url link="https://example.com" : Example Site] today.
    """

#h3: Mixed Inline Example

All inline formatting can appear together in a paragraph\:

#code: """
    A paragraph with #**"bold", #__"italic", and
    [#url link="https://example.com" text="a link"] all in one line.
    """

#h2: Lists

#h3: Unordered Lists

Wrap list items in \#ul. Each item uses \#* (or its alias \#li)\:

#code: """
    [#ul :
      #*: First item
      #*: Second item
      #*: Third item
    ]
    """

#h3: Ordered Lists

Use [#code"#ol"] instead of [#code"#ul"]\:

#code: """
    [#ol :
      #*: Step one
      #*: Step two
      #*: Step three
    ]
    """

#h3: Items with Formatting

List items can contain any inline macro\:

#code: """
    [#ul :
      #*: A #**"bold" item
      #*: An #__"italic" item
      #*: A [#url link="https://example.com" text="linked"] item
    ]
    """

#h3: Nested Lists

Use the bracketed form of [#code"#*"] and nest a list inside\:

#code: """
    [#ul :
      #*: Top-level item
      [#* : Item with sublist
        [#ul :
          #*: Nested A
          #*: Nested B
        ]
      ]
      #*: Another top-level item
    ]
    """

#h2: Tables

The simplest way to make a table is with pipe-delimited rows. The first row
becomes headers\:

#code: """
    #table:
      Name | Age | Status
      Alice | 30 | Active
      Bob | 25 | Inactive
    """

Cells can contain inline macros\:

#code: """
    #table:
      Feature | Supported
      Bold | [#**"Yes"]
      Links | [#url link="https://example.com" text="Yes"]
    """

For full control (colspan, custom structure), use the explicit form with
\#tr, \#th, and \#td\:

#code: """
    [#table :
      [#tr : [#th: Name] [#th: Age]]
      [#tr : [#td: Alice] [#td: 30]]
      [#tr : [#td span=2 : Total: 1 person]]
    ]
    """

#h2: Code Blocks

For inline code, use [#code"#code"]\:

#code: """
    Use the [#code : print()] function to output text.
    """

For code blocks, use a raw string body with an optional [#code"language"]
parameter\:

#code: """"
    [#code language=python : """
        def hello():
            print("Hello, world!")
        """]
    """"

The indentation of the closing [#code"\"\"\""] delimiter determines how much
leading whitespace is stripped from every line.

To show PicoDoc syntax itself without it being interpreted, use \#literal\:

#code: """"
    #literal"""
    The #b macro makes text bold.
    Use [#url link="..." text="..."] for links.
    """
    """"

#h2: User-Defined Macros

#h3: Simple Variables

Define a variable with [#code"#set"]. A macro with no parameters is just a
named value\:

#code: """
    [#set name=version : 1.0]
    The current version is #version.
    """

#h3: Macros with Arguments

Add parameters to create reusable templates. Mark required parameters with
? and provide defaults with \=value\:

#code: """
    [#set name=greeting target=? : Hello, #target!]
    [#greeting target=World]
    [#greeting target=Alice]
    """

#code: """
    [#set name=box style=default body=? : [#p : (#style) #body]]
    [#box : Content with default style.]
    [#box style=fancy : Content with fancy style.]
    """

#h3: Macros with Body

If your macro accepts a body, declare it as [#code"body=?"] (required) and
it must be the last parameter\:

#code: """
    [#set name=greeting target=? body=? : Dear #target, #body Kind regards.]
    [#greeting target=World : thank you for your support.]
    """

#h3: Out-of-Order Use

Macros can be used before they are defined. PicoDoc collects all definitions
first, then expands\:

#code: """
    #p: The project is #project-name.
    [#set name=project-name : PicoDoc]
    """

#h2: String Literals

#h3: Interpreted Strings

Delimited by single double quotes. Escape sequences are processed\:

#code: """
    "A simple string."
    "A tab:\there. A newline:\nSecond line."
    "A literal quote: \" inside."
    """

To embed macro calls inside a string, use [#code"\\[...\\]"] (code mode)\:

#code: """
    [#set name=version : 1.0]
    #p"PicoDoc version \[#version] is ready."
    """

#h3: Raw Strings

Delimited by three or more double quotes. Nothing inside is processed\:

#code: """"
    """This is raw: \n is literal, #name is not expanded."""
    """"

#h3: Whitespace Stripping

When a string starts on the next line after the delimiter and the closing
delimiter is on its own line, leading whitespace matching the closing
delimiter's indentation is stripped from all lines\:

#code: """
    [#code language=python : """
        def hello():
            print("world")
        """]
    """

The four spaces before each line are stripped, producing clean output.

#h2: Conditionals

PicoDoc provides three conditional macros\:

The \#ifeq macro tests string equality\:

#code: """
    [#set name=mode : draft]
    [#ifeq lhs=#mode rhs=draft : This document is a draft.]
    """

The \#ifne macro tests string inequality\:

#code: """
    [#ifne lhs=#mode rhs=production : Not yet published.]
    """

The \#ifset macro tests if a macro is defined\:

#code: """
    [#ifset name=env.author : Written by [#env.author].]
    """

A practical pattern is a draft watermark controlled by an environment
variable\:

#code: """
    [#ifeq lhs=[#env.mode] rhs=draft :
      #p: DRAFT — not for distribution.
    ]
    """

Then compile with [#code"uv run picodoc -e mode=draft doc.pdoc -o doc.html"].

#h2: Including Other Files

The [#code"#include"] macro inserts another file at the call site\:

#code: """
    [#include file="header.pdoc"]

    #h2: Main Content

    #p: The body of the document.

    [#include file="footer.pdoc"]
    """

This is the standard pattern for shared headers and footers across a set of
documents.

#h2: Environment Variables

The [#code"env.*"] namespace provides global values. They can be set from
three sources\:

#p: [#b : CLI]\:

#code: """
    uv run picodoc -e mode=draft -e author="Alice" doc.pdoc -o doc.html
    """

#p: [#b : Config file] ([#code"picodoc.toml"])\:

#code: """
    [env]
    mode = "draft"
    author = "Alice"
    """

#p: [#b : Document-level \#set]\:

#code: """
    [#set name=env.mode : draft]
    """

Document-level definitions have the highest precedence, then CLI, then config.

Access environment values as zero-argument macros\:

#code: """
    [#ifset name=env.author : Written by [#env.author].]
    """

#h2: CLI Quick Reference

#table:
  Flag | Description
  [#code"-o FILE"] | Write output to FILE instead of stdout
  [#code"-e NAME=VALUE"] | Set an environment variable (repeatable)
  [#code"--css FILE"] | Inject a CSS file into the head (repeatable)
  [#code"--js FILE"] | Inject a JS file into the head (repeatable)
  [#code"--meta NAME=VALUE"] | Add a meta tag (repeatable)
  [#code"--filter-path DIR"] | Extra filter search directory (repeatable)
  [#code"--filter-timeout SECS"] | Filter execution timeout (default\: 5.0)
  [#code"--config FILE"] | Config file path
  [#code"--watch"] | Watch for changes and recompile
  [#code"--debug"] | Dump the AST to stderr

#p: [#b : Watch mode] recompiles automatically whenever the input file changes\:

#code: """
    uv run picodoc --watch doc.pdoc -o doc.html
    """

#p: [#b : Exit codes]\:

#table:
  Code | Meaning
  0 | Success
  1 | Syntax error (lex or parse failure)
  2 | Evaluation error or invalid arguments

#h2: Configuration File

Place a [#code"picodoc.toml"] next to your input file. CLI flags override
config values.

#code: """
    [env]
    author = "Jane Doe"

    [css]
    files = ["style.css"]

    [meta]
    viewport = "width=device-width, initial-scale=1"
    """

#h2: Next Steps

You now know enough to write real documents in PicoDoc. For a complete and
systematic description of every feature, see the [#b : Language Reference]
in the file [#code"reference.pdoc"].

